  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14418v1</id>
    <title>Precision on Demand: Escalating Weight Bits at Uncertain Decoding Steps in a Small Local Model</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T17:57:51Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14418v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14418v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Local decoding usually fixes one weight precision for the whole generation. We study a different allocation: store 8-bit weights, decode with their top 4 bits, and redo a step at 8 bits only when the low-precision output distribution looks uncertain (entropy above a threshold or a small top-two margin). Quantization is simulated, the model is Qwen3-0.6B, and the benchmark is GSM8K. Cost is counted in weight bytes read per token. On 36724 replayed steps the entropy of the low pass ranks argmax flips with AUC 0.93. At a 15% escalation budget it catches 78% of flips and the margin gate 94%, against the 15% expected for a random gate of equal rate (a computed expectation, not a run). In 150 live problems all-high scores 64.0% and all-low 55.3% (McNemar p=0.053). An entropy gate with a 16% escalation rate scores 62.7% at 0.87 of the all-high weight bytes. Its paired difference is -1.3 (-8.0 to +5.3) points to all-high and +7.3 (+0.0 to +14.7) points to all-low (p=0.080), so the live data do not separate the gate from either end. Verified speculation reaches zero KL at similar bytes in the same cost model. A live random control, a thinking-mode phase rule, a second model and live KL were not run.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T17:57:51Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Mohammed Nayeem Khan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Logesh L</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Aliyas Ahmad Misri</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05862v1</id>
    <title>CARTBench: Rubric-Based Evaluation of Shopping Advice for Complex and Ambiguous Requests with Timelines</title>
    <updated>2026-11-04T02:52:07Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05862v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.05862v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Large language model (LLM) shopping assistants now recommend products in multi-turn conversations. However, reliable evaluation faces Data scarcity and Difficulty Standardizing Criteria. To address these obstacles, we introduce CARTBench for CART (Complex and Ambiguous Requests with Timelines). For Data scarcity, we curate cases from real commercial shopping needs across 17 retail domains, pairing timestamped dialogue with 1,603 product records. For Difficulty Standardizing Criteria, we develop case-specific rubrics guided by Ideal Response Principles, distilled from excellent replies manually selected from successfully completed orders. These principles also guide Principle-Guided CART Prompting, our structured recommendation strategy. The experimental benchmark contains 1,328 expert-annotated criteria across Core, Excellence, and Safety. To answer the CART question, we evaluate 7 flagship commercial API models, including GPT-6.1-Sol and Claude-Opus-5, and 35B+ parameters open-weight models. In the first full-suite evaluation under the frozen rubrics, 3 of 10 models exceed 90% on Core, while none exceeds 45% on Excellence. Therefore, we identify an understanding-to-execution gap between required behavior and high-quality purchase advice. To examine these limitations, we diagnose shared failures in product evidence and unresolved needs, linked to changing requirements and decisive unknowns. Code and data are available at https://github.com/Da1yuqin/CARTBench.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-04T02:52:07Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
