  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2608.22237v2</id>
    <title>Read Less, Solve More: Token-Efficient Sparse Reading of External Content for AI Agents</title>
    <updated>2026-11-07T06:27:54Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.22237v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.22237v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Web-facing AI agents repeatedly access webpages, documents, repositories, and tool responses, yet reading interfaces often expose entire objects when only sparse evidence is needed. This over-reading increases token and latency costs and can dilute relevant evidence. We present SparseRead, a reusable reading control layer that governs content admission without modifying the agent model. Its Read Intent Protocol tracks evolving needs and source coverage; Adaptive Reader Backends select how evidence is located and exposed; and the Budgeted Read Gate bounds cumulative raw exposure per episode with faithful full-view fallback. A unified Reader interface supports interchangeable evidence acquisition across heterogeneous resources. Across six models, including Claude Opus 5, and five scenarios, SparseRead reduces total tokens by up to 92.9% and wall time by up to 89.0%, preserving or improving task quality in 97% settings. On Web deep-search tasks, it reduces tokens by 31.0--43.0% and task completion time by 23.0--37.0%. Ablations validate the three designs, while integration with three agent frameworks demonstrates portability.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-08-23T06:24:26Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
