  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11306v2</id>
    <title>ScrapeBench: Evaluating Legal Compliance of AI Agents in Website Scraping</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T11:23:10Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11306v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.11306v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>AI agents deployed on the open web are becoming more capable of performing actions that may breach contractual provisions and violate computer security laws. In this paper, we introduce ScrapeBench, the first agentic benchmark for empirically evaluating the legal compliance of AI agents with website scraping policies, namely terms of service (ToS) and $\texttt{robots.txt}$ directives. Across widely used closed-weight and open-weight AI agents, we find that most agents exhibit low compliance rates (&lt; 5%) when instructed to scrape websites in our sample of 1,626 popular websites that explicitly prohibit scraping. In addition, we observe that even where AI agents actively check the applicable ToS and $\texttt{robots.txt}$, they continue to exhibit low compliance rates. Conversely, in our sample of 269 websites that do not prohibit scraping, we observe that agents sometimes refuse to scrape despite it being lawful, such as Claude Opus 4.6 exhibiting an over-refusal rate of 98.5%. Our human baseline experiments (n = 180 participants) suggest that humans exhibit compliance rates comparable to AI agents but over-refuse at much higher rates.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-08T11:19:18Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Camera-Ready for NeurIPS 2026 (Evaluations and Datasets Track)</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Joseph Marvin Imperial</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Daniel Slate</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Noam Kolt</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08379v1</id>
    <title>Module-Aware Optimization for Graph Neural Networks</title>
    <updated>2026-11-05T17:56:33Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08379v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.08379v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Different optimizers impose different geometries on parameter updates. Adaptive methods such as Adam and AdamW use coordinate-wise updates, while matrix-aware optimizers such as Muon utilize weight-matrix structure through orthogonalized updates. For Graph Neural Networks (GNNs), however, the effective geometry of its weight matrices is shaped not only by its learned channel-mixing weights, but also by the graph-propagated representations it acts on. Thus, even if an update is well normalized as a matrix, its effect on the layer output can be amplified when applied to graph-propagated features. To address this gap, we introduce GraphMuon, a graph-aware optimizer that adapts Muon's matrix updates to the covariance structure of graph-propagated layer inputs. GraphMuon performs orthogonalization in a metric defined by these propagated representations and normalizes step sizes according to the update's induced effect on the layer representation. We show that theoretically, vanilla Muon can suffer from graph-induced update amplification, while our GraphMuon can mitigate this effect. As such, our GraphMuon yields a graph-metric analogue of Muon's polar update step. Empirically, GraphMuon achieves $1.32\times$-$2.42\times$ validation-loss time-to-target speedups over AdamW on three large-scale OGB benchmarks. Across 15 dataset-architecture configurations spanning node classification, inductive graph classification, and graph regression, it achieves lower time-to-target in 13 cases, while maintaining competitive or superior predictive performance. Code is available at https://github.com/guy-hadad/GraphMuon.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-05T17:56:33Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>NeurIPS 2026</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Guy Hadad</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Haggai Roitman</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Moshe Eliasof</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Bracha Shapira</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13843v1</id>
    <title>PolyRoute: A Twelve-Language and Code-Mixed Benchmark for Typed-Decision Routing, with Baselines and a Leaderboard</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T10:04:27Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13843v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13843v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Typed-decision encoders answer a question about a text in one forward pass, but their behaviour outside English is rarely measured on a fixed yardstick. We release PolyRoute: 322 native-language support messages in 12 languages and 9 writing systems, 156 code-mixed or romanised messages (Hinglish, Spanglish, Arabizi), 967 optional native training rows, and a protocol for a three-way department-routing decision. Labels come from Qwen3.8-27B with a blind second pass (91% of candidates kept); an independent model, gemma4-31b, agrees on 97.2% of test items, and the largest character 5-gram similarity between any test text and any training text is 0.33. A leaderboard of 13 systems gives 61.2% for the English-root checkpoint, 77.3% for the shipped multilingual one and 96.6% for the best fine-tune; TF-IDF with logistic regression reaches 91.9%. On four withheld languages the three multilingual systems score 77%, 90% and 93%. On code-mixed text the English root (85%) is nominally ahead of the shipped multilingual checkpoint (79%; p=0.134). The split resolves differences only above about 4 points. All texts and labels are LLM-generated.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T10:04:27Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>25 pages, 18 figures, 26 tables</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Mohammed Nayeem Khan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Sarun Kumar K S</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13766v1</id>
    <title>FAME-QP: A GPU-Accelerated Quadratic-Programming Solver for Real-Time Model Predictive Control</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T09:06:55Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13766v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13766v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We present FAME-QP, a GPU-accelerated solver for dense quadratic programs arising in linear model predictive control. FAME-QP combines constraint-dependent operator representations with fused single- and two-kernel iterations replayed through CUDA graphs, automatically selecting the execution configuration through device calibration and problem-specific timing. It probes a cached operator bank to select the initial penalty and updates operators lazily when problem matrices change. Numerical benchmarks and simulated humanoid and quadrotor control experiments demonstrate reduced latency relative to competing GPU solvers while maintaining solution accuracy and control performance.</summary>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.GR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="eess.SY" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T09:06:55Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.RO"/>
    <author>
      <name>Santosh Mohan Rajkumar</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Debdipta Goswami</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Stephanie Stockar</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08723v1</id>
    <title>Divide, Diagnose, and Compose: Optimizing Agentic Pipelines and Harnesses</title>
    <updated>2026-11-05T21:16:05Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08723v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.08723v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Building agentic pipelines that achieve high task performance while keeping cost and latency low requires jointly optimizing heterogeneous and coupled design choices, including prompts, topology, model selection, and tool policies. Manually searching this combinatorial design space is expensive and time-consuming, while existing automated optimizers typically operate within a predefined subset of this space, modify the pipeline monolithically, or provide little structured evidence for pipeline changes. To mitigate these challenges, we introduce the Decomposed Credit-Assignment Framework (DeCAF), which adapts the design space to the given benchmark and pipeline, optimizes each dimension separately, and generates reasoning traces for every decision. Given a task specification, benchmark examples, and a seed pipeline, DeCAF identifies relevant optimization dimensions and generates diverse experimental hypotheses for each. Independent coding-agent sessions then optimize each dimension under a restricted edit scope, jointly evaluating performance, inference cost, and latency while recording all hypotheses, edits, and outcomes of each experiment. A final synthesis session combines compatible findings across all dimensions and uses a component-level trajectory evaluator, which derives role-specific rubrics from each agent's instructions, to localize underperforming components and guide cross-dimension revisions. Across a diverse suite of agentic benchmarks, DeCAF improves average task performance by 2.58 percentage points, while reducing inference cost by 4.30% and end-to-end latency by 42.45% compared to the best overall baseline.</summary>
    <category term="cs.MA" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-05T21:16:05Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.MA"/>
    <author>
      <name>Divij Handa</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Christopher Tensmeyer</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jihyung Kil</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Vlad I. Morariu</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12304v1</id>
    <title>Evolving Infra: Agent-Adaptive LLM Serving at Scale</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T12:26:02Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12304v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12304v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Agentic workloads are a major source of LLM inference demand. Existing prefill--decode (PD) disaggregated serving systems offer limited flexibility for runtime adaptation of compute allocation and KV cache management, and rely on coarse signals that only partly capture the diverse and dynamic agent workload. These limitations lead to load imbalance between prefill and decode nodes and eviction of KV caches that agents will reuse. We present Evolving Infra, an agent-adaptive serving framework that adapts compute allocation to agent workloads and KV cache management to agent context management at runtime. We decouple compute allocation from key--value (KV) cache management, allowing nodes to switch efficiently between prefill and decode. Building on this design, an online planner matches the PD split to workload composition, while an agent-aware distributed KV manager guides cache eviction and transfer with signals from agent context management. Across coding, math, and computer-use tasks with GLM-5.2 and Kimi K2.6 on up to 256 GB300 GPUs, Evolving Infra reduces mean end-to-end request latency and P95 TTFT by up to 4.1x and 14x over Dynamo, NVIDIA's production framework.</summary>
    <category term="cs.DC" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T12:26:02Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.DC"/>
    <author>
      <name>Hao Kang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Weili Xu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ziyang Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zheng Du</name>
    </author>
