  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12545v1</id>
    <title>Teamknitt: Configuring, Observing and Intervening in LLM-Agent Teams through the Lens of Dynamical-Systems and Human-Team Theory</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T15:15:39Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12545v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12545v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Most frameworks for teams of large language model (LLM) agents fix the team's shape inside the software: who talks to whom, how much is passed along, and what a human can do once a run has started. Human-team research has long asked those questions: where team phenomena live (in members or in their interaction), how team-level properties arise, and what separates what a team does from how a team is. I propose using that theory as the organizing lens for configuring, observing and intervening in LLM-agent teams, without assuming its findings transfer.
  I introduce teamknitt, a framework and open reference implementation in which a user configures a team of 1-16 AI agents for task completion, solution generation, achieving a goal or a research mission; selects one of fourteen deterministic routing topologies or a custom directed graph; chooses a sharing profile that limits what may travel along permitted routes; and binds sources, representations, work dependencies, handoffs and an expertise directory. The recorded process can be observed through non-system views (member reports and behavior) and system views (distributed problem-solving, differentiated transactive memory, interactive coordination, team dynamics). Interventions are explicit and human-led: reviewed reconfiguration between runs, planned disturbances, and private feedback, pause, resume or stop applied at dependency-wave boundaries, with "queued", "applied", "assembled", "understood" and "improved" kept as separate claims. Emergent states such as trust and shared understanding are declared unmeasured.
  This is an idea and system-description paper with falsifiable hypotheses on topology, information flow and intervention timing, including whether the fractal scaling of communication output between members, and between members on different models, relates to task and team performance. No comparative result is reported.</summary>
    <category term="physics.soc-ph" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.MA" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T15:15:39Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>17 pages, 3 figures, 6 tables. Reference implementation v0.9.0: https://github.com/JeranRolevs/teamknitt</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="physics.soc-ph"/>
    <author>
      <name>Goran Mitevski</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13410v1</id>
    <title>SteerDistill: Quantizing LLM Agents via Closed-Loop Teacher Steering</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T00:45:58Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13410v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13410v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Low-bit quantization reduces the inference cost of LLM agents but can compromise task success. Examining failed rollouts, we find that quantized agents overlook task requirements or environmental conditions, producing environment transitions that diverge from successful full-precision references. Correcting these behavioral divergences remains difficult: offline data may miss the student's failure contexts, while its own rollouts may provide few correct responses in those contexts. We introduce SteerDistill, a quantization-aware distillation method in which a full-precision teacher guides the student to revise failed responses and iteratively refines its guidance using feedback from student execution. This closed-loop teacher steering produces locally verified behavioral corrections. We internalize these corrections by training the student without guidance to match the steered student's predictions at corrected turns and the full-precision teacher's at other turns, enabling unassisted inference. Across five agentic benchmarks and three models, SteerDistill exceeds the strongest quantized baseline by 1.67--2.18 points in average score under W4A16 and stays within one point of BF16. On $τ^2$-Bench, it outperforms QeRL by 1.84 percentage points in success rate using 67% fewer training GPU hours and produces shorter successful trajectories than the quantized baselines.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T00:45:58Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Preprint</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Genming Cui</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xiangyu Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zixu Hao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xin Ding</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yunxin Liu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ting Cao</name>
    </author>
