  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13758v1</id>
    <title>Mixed Signals: Weight-Space Programming for EEG and ECG</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T08:59:09Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13758v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13758v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Weight-space programming allows language models to read a trained neural network's weights and edit them. This opens the door to ``training'' by reasoning rather than through gradient descent. Prior work has demonstrated its functionality and promise for game-playing networks and fixing specific failures in language models, even extending to diffusion models. In this work, we present its applicability to models trained on biosignal data, specifically EEG and ECG data. We show that frontier models are not only able to debug specific faults planted in biosignal-based models without being told what these faults are, but are even capable of writing all the weights of a network from scratch, given only the architecture, the task, and a minimal set of training subjects. In particular, in one set of experiments, a language model was able to match standard gradient-descent training using $6\times$ fewer training subjects, through reasoning alone and without any gradient descent step. Networks written by Claude Opus 5.5 also scored higher than classic hand-crafted EEG pipelines (alpha band power, and common spatial patterns with linear discriminant analysis) when built from the same 5 subjects. We also highlight some of the limitations, which include the inability of language models to improve already well-trained classification models, and their incapacity to reliably recognize, from the weights alone, the task a network was trained on. Overall, our results indicate that weight-space programming can be useful for small networks, such as those deployed on edge devices and wearables, but the concept still requires further investigation before it can be used in practice.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="eess.SP" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T08:59:09Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>9 pages, 7 figures, 5 tables. Project website: https://daravaram.github.io/BioWSP</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Dara Varam</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Taraneh Aminosharieh Najafi</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>David Atienza</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14413v1</id>
    <title>Bits or permutations? Spending a fixed inference budget on weight precision or on option-order averaging in small local language models</title>
    <updated>2026-11-10T17:53:44Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14413v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14413v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Local language models can save memory by storing weights in fewer bits, and they can reduce sensitivity to the order of answer options by averaging a few reorderings. We ask whether the first makes the second worse, and which is the cheaper way to spend a fixed budget. On Qwen3-0.6B and Qwen3-1.7B with simulated round-to-nearest weight quantization (8, 6, 4 and 3 bits, fp16 reference), read from the logits of the letters A to D on ARC-Easy, ARC-Challenge, MMLU and HellaSwag (300 items each), 6 bits lose no accuracy against fp16 (they score +1.8 (+1.0 to +2.5) and +0.9 (+0.3 to +1.5) points higher, which we do not explain), and 8 bits change it by -0.7 (-1.1 to -0.3) and -0.4 (-0.7 to -0.2) points. At 4 bits the answer-slot skew rises from 0.15 to 0.53 (0.6B) and from 0.07 to 0.12 (1.7B); Gaussian noise at the same weight SNR gives 0.15 and 0.37 (two draws) and 0.19 (one draw), so the control is inconclusive on whether rounding adds slot skew beyond equal-power noise. Flip rates rise but stay below an accuracy-matched baseline of independent uniform errors. Averaging four cyclic orders recovers +12.6 points at 4 bits for 0.6B (+5.3 at fp16). We price bits and passes in one machine-independent unit, weight bytes read times passes. In cost-matched pairs, extra orders beat extra bits only when the weights have 6 or more bits, and the decision table reduces to: use 6-bit weights and spend the rest of the budget on up to four orders, one pass each. Everything is simulated on two small models with the simplest quantizer; a title-level search found no earlier study of quantization together with option order.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-10T17:53:44Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>25 pages, 15 figures, 9 tables</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Mohammed Nayeem Khan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Logesh L</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Aliyas Ahmad Misri</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12869v1</id>
    <title>Where Does Alignment Emerge and What Does It Mean? A Closer Look at the Platonic Representation Hypothesis</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T18:53:57Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12869v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12869v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>The Platonic Representation Hypothesis (PRH) suggests that neural networks are converging toward similar representation spaces. As language models become more capable, their representations are aligning with those of vision models. However, recent work has questioned whether this trend continues to hold for newer language models. Moreover, it has been shown that representation similarity metrics can be confounded by model depth and width, requiring appropriate baselines for interpretation. In this work, we first investigate where alignment emerges between unimodal models across model layers and representation dimensions. Next, we find that the PRH capability-alignment trend persists for newer models as long as the captions are sufficiently clean and informative. Finally, we contextualize cross-modal alignment, introducing unimodal and multimodal baselines on various datasets. Our results show that the cross-modal representational alignment of unimodal models can surpass that of explicitly aligned vision-language models, but remains substantially behind the alignment observed for models of the same modality. Code is available at https://github.com/ExplainableML/prh-closer-look.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T18:53:57Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>NeurIPS 2026 Workshop on Neural Network Artifacts as a New Data Modality (Spotlight)</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Julia Herbst</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Simon Roschmann</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Quentin Bouniot</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zeynep Akata</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
